# =============================================================================
# Environment Variables Configuration
# =============================================================================
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the values.
#
# Conventions:
#   - Required vars are uncommented. The app will not start without them.
#   - Optional vars are commented out. Uncomment and fill in to enable.
#   - All values are double-quoted (handles spaces and special characters).
#
# GitHub annotations:
#   [SECRET]     - Store in GitHub Secrets (sensitive, encrypted)
#                  Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → Repository secrets
#                  AND Settings → Secrets and variables → Dependabot → Repository secrets
#   [VAR]        - Store in GitHub Variables (non-sensitive, visible in logs)
#                  Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → Repository variables
#                  (Dependabot inherits Repository variables automatically — no duplication needed)
#   [LOCAL]      - Only needed for local development, not in CI or CF
#
# Cloudflare annotations:
#   [CF-BUILD]   - CF dashboard -> Workers & Pages -> <app> -> Settings ->
#                  Build -> Variables and secrets
#                  Required during `yarn build` (drizzle-kit migrate + Vite bundle)
#                  NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars must be here — Vite bakes them in at build time
#   [CF-RUNTIME] - Available to the deployed Worker at request time via process.env.
#                  Route by sensitivity:
#                    - Sensitive → `wrangler secret put <NAME>` (or CF dashboard
#                      Variables and secrets → Secret). Persists across deploys.
#                    - Non-sensitive → "vars" block in wrangler.jsonc. Committed
#                      to git, survives deploys.
#                  Do NOT use CF dashboard plaintext (non-secret) vars for runtime —
#                  they get wiped on each CF Workers Builds deploy.
#
# See lib/env.config.ts for the canonical source of truth.
# Run `npm run env:check` to validate your configuration.
#
# IMPORTANT: when you add, rename, or remove any [CF-RUNTIME] var, you must also
# review and update the `vars` block in wrangler.jsonc.
#
# Values do NOT need to match between .env.local and wrangler.jsonc — they are
# different environments. .env.local holds local-dev values; wrangler.jsonc holds
# the production CF Workers values. Most [CF-RUNTIME] vars *should* differ
# (e.g. BETTER_AUTH_URL, EMAIL_ENV). `npm run env:check` only compares the
# set of keys, never their values.
#
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# DATABASE (Supabase + Drizzle)
# https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/connecting-to-postgres
# =============================================================================
#
# Supabase provides three connection string types — use the right one:
#
#   DIRECT (unpooled, port 5432):
#     postgresql://postgres:[PASSWORD]@db.[project-ref].supabase.co:5432/postgres
#
#   SESSION POOLER (port 5432 via pooler):
#     postgresql://postgres.[project-ref]:[PASSWORD]@aws-0-[region].pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
#   TRANSACTION POOLER (port 6543) — DO NOT USE:
#     Transaction mode breaks prepared statements used by postgres.js.
#
# In Cloudflare Workers production, POSTGRES_URL is automatically overridden
# by the HYPERDRIVE binding — it is only used for local dev and CI.
# The Hyperdrive binding ID is NOT an env var; configure it in wrangler.jsonc:
#   "hyperdrive": [{ "binding": "HYPERDRIVE", "id": "<your-hyperdrive-id>" }]
#
# Setup: Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> Database -> Connection string

# [SECRET] [CF-BUILD] [CF-RUNTIME] App runtime connection — use the DIRECT or SESSION POOLER string.
POSTGRES_URL="postgresql://postgres:[PASSWORD]@db.[project-ref].supabase.co:5432/postgres"

# [SECRET] [CF-BUILD] Migration-only connection — always use the DIRECT (unpooled) string.
# Required by `npx drizzle-kit migrate` which runs during build and in CI. Never used by the app itself.
# drizzle.config.ts falls back to POSTGRES_URL if this is unset.
# TIP: point the GitHub Secret value at a test Supabase project so CI migrations don't run against production.
POSTGRES_DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:[PASSWORD]@db.[project-ref].supabase.co:5432/postgres"

# =============================================================================
# AUTHENTICATION (better-auth)
# =============================================================================

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] better-auth encryption secret — generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET="your_better_auth_secret_here"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Base URL for better-auth callbacks.
# NOTE: Vite bakes process.env.BETTER_AUTH_URL as undefined (CF-RUNTIME vars are
# not present during build). auth.ts falls back to NEXT_PUBLIC_HOST_URL instead.
# Keep this set in CF-RUNTIME for any future direct reads; for origin checking
# NEXT_PUBLIC_HOST_URL is the effective value.
BETTER_AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# =============================================================================
# HOST CONFIGURATION
# =============================================================================

# [VAR] [CF-BUILD] Public-facing site URL — baked into the Vite bundle at build time
NEXT_PUBLIC_HOST_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Canonical fediverse identity domain.
# This is the domain in @user@domain handles and the origin of every ActivityPub
# actor/status/follow URI this instance mints. Remote servers store those URIs as
# permanent keys, so it is effectively WRITE-ONCE: changing it after an actor has
# federated orphans that actor, and the Move migration that repairs it carries
# followers but NOT posts.
#
# Kept separate from NEXT_PUBLIC_HOST_URL on purpose, so the UI can move to a
# dedicated social hostname (e.g. social.panamia.club) without re-minting every
# identity. Whatever host serves the app, THIS domain must answer
# /.well-known/webfinger and serve /p/[user] as ActivityPub JSON.
#
# Unset falls back to the NEXT_PUBLIC_HOST_URL hostname, which preserves the
# behaviour deployments had before this variable existed. That fallback is the
# wrong default for local development: it resolves to "localhost" while every
# seeded actor is minted at pana.social, so getActorByScreenname matches
# nothing and /panas, /groups and the feed's suggestions strip all render
# EMPTY — indistinguishable from "not built yet". Production pins this in
# wrangler.jsonc; local dev has to pin it here. Note this is the opposite of
# PANAVERSE_COOKIE_DOMAIN below, whose production value must be overridden
# locally rather than copied.
FEDERATION_DOMAIN="pana.social"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Registrable domain the panaverse surfaces hang off.
# Each surface in lib/panaverse/surfaces.ts is a subdomain of this — the main
# site at the apex, Pana Social at social.<root> — and the Worker routes a
# surface hostname to that surface's front door. Only the root path is routed;
# every other path stays reachable from every hostname, so /api and
# /.well-known behave the same no matter which surface a request lands on.
#
# Hosts that match nothing (localhost, *.workers.dev previews) fall back to the
# main site, which is exactly how things behaved before surfaces existed. For
# local testing, social.localhost:3000 resolves to the social surface.
# PANAVERSE_ROOT_DOMAIN="pana.social"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Set to "1" once every surface subdomain resolves AND is
# bound to this Worker. That is Cloudflare dashboard work, not a repo change —
# see docs/DOMAINS.md.
#
# While unset, cross-surface links, metadataBase and canonical URLs all stay on
# the hostname already being served, so Pana Social is reached at /s. A link to
# a host with no DNS record is a dead end rather than a slower route, which is
# why unset is the safe default and why this ships off.
#
# Routing does not depend on this. Every surface route answers on every
# hostname, so turning it on moves the front door without moving any route.
# Local dev is exempt — *.localhost resolves without DNS, so social.localhost
# keeps reaching the social surface either way.
# PANAVERSE_SUBDOMAINS="1"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Cookie domain shared across the panaverse surfaces, so one
# sign-in carries between pana.social and social.pana.social.
#
# Production sets this to ".pana.social" in wrangler.jsonc. It must be the
# registrable domain (leading dot). Note the surface root is also the fediverse
# identity domain, so ".pana.social" is sent to relay.pana.social too — scope
# anything issued there accordingly.
#
# LOCAL DEV MUST OVERRIDE IT TO EMPTY, as below. Local dev reads wrangler.jsonc
# vars, so the production value applies here too, and Chrome refuses a
# .pana.social cookie on a localhost host — sign-in silently stops working.
# There is no working local value: Chrome also rejects Domain=localhost, since
# single-label domains can't carry a Domain attribute. In both cases the server
# still sends Set-Cookie, so curl reports success while the browser stores
# nothing — test cookie changes in a browser, not with curl.
#
# Empty means host-only cookies: localhost and social.localhost hold separate
# sessions in dev. Measured on both surfaces, with controls:
#   - Signing in on either host DOES create a real session on that host.
#     /api/auth/get-session returns the user with the cookie and null without
#     it, and the www host returns null for a social cookie.
#   - But magic-link verify ALWAYS redirects to the www host, whichever host
#     served it, because callbackURL is relative and resolves against the www
#     base URL. A sign-in begun on social.localhost therefore dumps you on
#     localhost, where you are signed out. The social session does exist; you
#     cannot see it until you navigate back to social.localhost yourself.
# So the failure is cosmetic-looking but misleading: the sign-in worked and
# the redirect makes it look like it did not. Do not read that bounce as
# "social cannot hold a session" -- that was measured and is false.
#
# A registrable dev domain (e.g. .localtest.me) does keep the leading dot and
# is visible across subdomains, which would restore one shared session -- but
# the dev server rejects that host with a 403, so it needs host-allowlisting
# plus PANAVERSE_ROOT_DOMAIN/NEXT_PUBLIC_HOST_URL changes before it works.
#
# If you run `yarn dev:setup`, the generated .dev.vars REPLACES .env.local as
# the dev env source (the server logs "Using secrets defined in .dev.vars").
# scripts/setup-dev-vars.ts copies this key through, including when empty, so
# the override survives — but only keys listed in that script's KEYS array do.
PANAVERSE_COOKIE_DOMAIN=

# =============================================================================
# ADMIN
# =============================================================================

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Comma-separated list of admin email addresses
ADMIN_EMAILS="admin1@example.com,admin2@example.com"

# =============================================================================
# OAUTH PROVIDERS - Google
# https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
# =============================================================================

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Google OAuth client ID
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="your_google_client_id_here"

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Google OAuth client secret
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="your_google_client_secret_here"

# =============================================================================
# OAUTH PROVIDERS - Apple
# https://developer.apple.com/account/resources/identifiers/list/serviceId
# =============================================================================

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Apple OAuth client ID (Services ID)
# APPLE_CLIENT_ID="your_apple_client_id_here"

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Apple OAuth client secret (signed JWT)
# APPLE_CLIENT_SECRET="your_apple_client_secret_here"

# =============================================================================
# OAUTH PROVIDERS - Wikimedia
# https://meta.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:OAuthConsumerRegistration
# =============================================================================

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Wikimedia OAuth client ID
# WIKIMEDIA_CLIENT_ID="your_wikimedia_client_id_here"

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Wikimedia OAuth client secret
# WIKIMEDIA_CLIENT_SECRET="your_wikimedia_client_secret_here"

# =============================================================================
# OAUTH PROVIDERS - Mastodon
# Create an application on your Mastodon instance
# =============================================================================

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Mastodon instance URL for OAuth (default: https://mastodon.social)
# MASTODON_INSTANCE="https://mastodon.social"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Mastodon OAuth client ID
# MASTODON_CLIENT_ID="your_mastodon_client_id_here"

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Mastodon OAuth client secret
# MASTODON_CLIENT_SECRET="your_mastodon_client_secret_here"

# =============================================================================
# OAUTH PROVIDERS - UI Visibility
# Uncomment and set to "true" to show the sign-in button for each provider.
# Also requires the credentials above to be set.
# =============================================================================
#
# These keep the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix for continuity, but they are ordinary
# RUNTIME vars: app/signin/page.tsx reads them on the server and passes the
# result to the client component as a prop. Set them in CF Runtime variables —
# or better, in wrangler.jsonc, where they survive Workers Builds deploys.
#
# Do NOT move them to CF Build variables. See docs/SIGNIN.md for why the
# distinction decides whether the buttons render at all.

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Show Google sign-in button (default: false)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_ENABLED="true"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Show Apple sign-in button (default: false)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APPLE_ENABLED="true"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Show Wikimedia sign-in button (default: false)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WIKIMEDIA_ENABLED="true"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Show Mastodon sign-in button (default: false)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MASTODON_ENABLED="true"

# =============================================================================
# OAUTH PROVIDERS - Trust Level
# Values: "trusted" | "verification-required" | "disabled"
#
# - trusted: Provider verifies email ownership (auto-claim enabled)
# - verification-required: User must verify email before sign-in completes
# - disabled: Provider button greyed out on sign-in page
#
# Defaults apply if unset: Google=trusted, Apple=trusted,
# Wikimedia=verification-required, Mastodon=trusted
# =============================================================================

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Google OAuth trust level
# OAUTH_GOOGLE="trusted"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Apple OAuth trust level
# OAUTH_APPLE="trusted"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Wikimedia OAuth trust level
# OAUTH_WIKIMEDIA="verification-required"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Mastodon.social OAuth trust level
# OAUTH_MASTODON_SOCIAL="trusted"

# =============================================================================
# TURNSTILE (Cloudflare)
# https://dash.cloudflare.com/?to=/:account/turnstile
# =============================================================================

# [VAR] [CF-BUILD] Cloudflare Turnstile site key (public, baked into bundle at build time)
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY="your_turnstile_site_key_here"

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Cloudflare Turnstile secret key
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY="your_turnstile_secret_key_here"

# =============================================================================
# MAPS (Esri active; Stadia parked)
# =============================================================================
#
# The directory search map and the profile location map draw their basemap
# tiles from Esri's World Street Map, which needs no key and no account.
# Leave this unset; nothing reads it while Esri is the active provider.
#
# Stadia's Alidade Smooth is the style we want and the code to use it is still
# in lib/map-tiles.tsx, but the account is not authorised: every request 401s,
# by domain auth and by API key alike, including on unrelated endpoints. Fix
# that in the dashboard first, then swap the two exports at the bottom of
# lib/map-tiles.tsx.
#
#   1. Sign up at https://client.stadiamaps.com/signup/
#   2. Create a property, then add your domain(s) under Authentication
#   3. Confirm a bare curl returns a real tile before trusting it — a 401
#      still comes back as a valid PNG, so check the image, not the status
#
# If you do set a key, note it is public by design: it is visible in every tile
# URL. Its protection is the domain allowlist in the dashboard, not secrecy.
# Browser traffic should use domain auth instead; a key is only for callers
# that send no Origin/Referer, such as server-side or native requests.

# [VAR] [CF-BUILD] Stadia Maps API key (public, baked into bundle at build time)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STADIA_API_KEY="your_stadia_api_key_here"

# =============================================================================
# EMAIL (Cloudflare Email Sending)
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/email-service/
# =============================================================================

# [VAR] [CF-BUILD] Sender email address (From: header). Non-secret — visible in every email.
# EMAIL_SENDER_ADDRESS="hola@pana.social"

# [VAR] [CF-BUILD] Sender display name
# EMAIL_SENDER_NAME="Pana MIA"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Email environment — "PROD" enables real sends, anything else logs to console (default: DEV)
# EMAIL_ENV="PROD"

# =============================================================================
# OBJECT STORAGE (Cloudflare R2)
# https://dash.cloudflare.com/?to=/:account/r2
# =============================================================================

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Cloudflare account ID (used for R2 S3-compatible endpoint)
R2_ACCOUNT_ID="your_cloudflare_account_id"

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] R2 API token key ID (create under R2 → Manage R2 API Tokens)
# https://dash.cloudflare.com/?to=/:account/r2/api-tokens
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID="your_r2_access_key_id"

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] R2 API token secret
# https://dash.cloudflare.com/?to=/:account/r2/api-tokens
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="your_r2_secret_access_key"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] R2 bucket name (e.g. panamia-media)
R2_BUCKET_NAME="your_r2_bucket_name"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Public base URL for the R2 bucket (r2.dev subdomain or custom domain)
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/buckets/public-buckets/
R2_PUBLIC_URL="https://your-bucket.r2.dev"

# =============================================================================
# STRIPE (Payments — server-side only)
# https://dashboard.stripe.com/apikeys
# =============================================================================
# Stripe.js (@stripe/stripe-js) is NOT loaded on the client. The donate flow
# creates a Checkout Session server-side and redirects to Stripe-hosted checkout.
# No client-side publishable key is needed.

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Stripe secret key
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY="sk_test_your_stripe_secret_key"

# =============================================================================
# CLOUDFLARE STREAM (Events livestreaming setup)
# https://dash.cloudflare.com -> Stream -> Live Inputs
# =============================================================================

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Cloudflare account ID
# CF_ACCOUNT_ID="your_cloudflare_account_id"

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Cloudflare Stream API token (needs Live Inputs write permission)
# CF_STREAM_API_TOKEN="your_stream_api_token"

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Secret used to verify Cloudflare Stream webhook requests
# Configure in CF dashboard → Stream → Webhooks → Signing Secret
# CF_STREAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET="your_stream_webhook_secret"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] Customer subdomain code for Stream embed URLs
# Found in CF dashboard → Stream → Overview — the customer-XXXX part of the embed URL
# CF_STREAM_CUSTOMER_CODE="your_customer_code"

# =============================================================================
# GOHIGHLEVEL (CRM)
# https://app.gohighlevel.com -> Settings -> Integrations -> Private Integrations
# =============================================================================

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] GHL private integration API key
# Create in GHL → Settings → Integrations → Private Integrations
# GHL_API_KEY="your_ghl_api_key"

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] GHL sub-account/location ID
# Shown in the GHL dashboard URL and in Settings.
# Not sensitive, but stored as a CF Workers secret (via `wrangler secret put`) for
# symmetry with GHL_API_KEY. Alternatively declare in wrangler.jsonc "vars".
# GHL_LOCATION_ID="your_ghl_location_id"

# [VAR] [CF-RUNTIME] GHL workflow UUID for the "Trigger test workflow" button in Advanced Settings
# Copy from the workflow URL in GHL → Automation → Workflows. Leave unset in production.
# GHL_WORKFLOW_TEST_ID="xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx"

# =============================================================================
# DMCA AGENT (Legal)
# https://dmca.copyright.gov — Register/renew designated agent ($6, every 3 years)
# =============================================================================
#
# These values are rendered into legal/dmca/policy.html and other legal pages.
# Use a registered agent service address — not a personal address.

# [VAR] [CF-BUILD] DMCA agent full name
# DMCA_AGENT_NAME="Your Registered Agent Name"

# [VAR] [CF-BUILD] DMCA agent email for takedown notices
# DMCA_AGENT_EMAIL="dmca@panamia.club"

# [VAR] [CF-BUILD] DMCA agent physical address (registered agent service)
# DMCA_AGENT_ADDRESS="123 Agent St, Suite 100, City, ST 00000"

# [VAR] [CF-BUILD] DMCA agent phone number
# DMCA_AGENT_PHONE="+1-000-000-0000"

# [VAR] [CF-BUILD] Date of DMCA agent registration with Copyright Office (YYYY-MM-DD)
# DMCA_REGISTRATION_DATE="2026-01-01"

# [VAR] [CF-BUILD] Next renewal date (3 years from registration) (YYYY-MM-DD)
# DMCA_RENEWAL_DATE="2029-01-01"

# =============================================================================
# DEVELOPMENT / TESTING
# =============================================================================

# [LOCAL] Development email redirect (safety net)
# When set, ALL outbound emails are sent here instead of real recipients.
# Prevents accidentally emailing real users during development.
# DEV_RECEIVER_EMAIL="your_dev_email@example.com"

# [VAR] Set to "1" for verbose per-request logging: every SQL statement
# (lib/db.ts) and the session-resolution breadcrumb (auth.ts).
#
# Off by default because these fire on every request and Workers Logs bills
# per event past the included allowance. Errors are logged either way, via the
# observability block in wrangler.jsonc -- this only controls the chatter on
# the successful path. Turn it on while investigating, then turn it back off.
#
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/observability/logs/
# DEBUG_REQUEST_LOG="1"

# =============================================================================
# HARD-CODED DOMAIN REFERENCES
# =============================================================================
#
# The string "panamia.club" appears in three categories in the source code.
# If this project is ever forked or the domain changes, update accordingly.
#
# 1. PRODUCTION DETECTION — intentional comparisons against "panamia.club"
#    to distinguish the production deployment from preview/workers.dev builds:
#      app/layout.tsx                          host.includes('panamia.club')
#      app/api/auth/verify-recaptcha/route.ts  host.includes('panamia.club')
#      components/SignupModal.tsx              NEXT_PUBLIC_HOST_URL.includes(...)
#
# 2. SITE_URL FALLBACKS — already read NEXT_PUBLIC_HOST_URL at runtime;
#    "https://panamia.club" is only a safe default if the env var is unset:
#      app/sitemap.ts
#      app/robots.ts
#      app/feed.xml/route.ts
#      app/feed.json/route.ts
#      app/feed/author/[screenname]/route.ts
#      app/feed/tag/[tag]/route.ts
#      app/feed/type/[type]/route.ts
#      app/api/create-checkout-session/route.ts  (Stripe origin fallback)
#
#    Federation identity is NO LONGER in this category: lib/federation/domain.ts
#    reads FEDERATION_DOMAIN and only falls back to the NEXT_PUBLIC_HOST_URL
#    hostname when that is unset. Pin FEDERATION_DOMAIN in production so moving
#    the UI to another hostname cannot re-mint actor URIs.
#
# 3. CONTENT LITERALS — correct to hard-code; not env-var controlled:
#      auth.ts                          email templates (footer, contact links)
#      app/doc/terms-and-conditions/    legal document text
#      app/form/become-a-pana/          error message contact email
#      app/api/createExpressProfile/    error message contact email
#      app/donation/confirmation/       contact email
#      app/links/page.tsx               mailto link
#      components/CallToActionBar.tsx   "Visit Panamia Club" link (prod URL from preview)
#      components/MainFooter.tsx        Instagram / GitHub social links
#      app/.well-known/nodeinfo/2.0/   software.name "panamia-club" in NodeInfo response

# =============================================================================
# NOSTR / RESILIENCE RELAY (panamia-nosflare)
# =============================================================================
# On article/event publish, panamia POSTs the event to the panamia-nosflare
# relay Worker's /internal/* endpoints. The relay signs a kind-30023 (NIP-23,
# articles) or kind-31923 (NIP-52, events) event with its own key, persists it
# to the home relay (NOSTR_HOME_RELAY), then fans out to any additional mirror
# relays in NOSTR_*_CROSSPOST_LIST (both configured on the relay, not here).
#
# In production both Workers are bound via Service Binding (RELAY → panamia-
# nosflare, in wrangler.jsonc), so RELAY_INTERNAL_URL is only consulted in plain
# Node dev (server.js) where there's no env.RELAY. All values are OPTIONAL —
# crosspost is best-effort and no-ops when unset.

# [SECRET] [CF-RUNTIME] Shared secret sent as X-Crosspost-Auth on internal relay
# calls; must match the relay Worker's CROSSPOST_AUTH_TOKEN. One token is shared
# by all instances/partners allowed to publish under the relay's pubkey — rotate
# on the relay + redistribute to revoke a publisher. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# CROSSPOST_AUTH_TOKEN=""

# [VAR] [LOCAL] HTTP fallback to the relay's internal endpoint, used only when
# the RELAY Service Binding is unavailable (plain Node dev). Ignored on CF.
# RELAY_INTERNAL_URL="https://relay.pana.social/internal/articles/crosspost"

# [VAR] This instance's authoritative home relay. An event's nostr_event_id is
# recorded ONLY when THIS relay accepts the crosspost — URLs in the relay's
# CROSSPOST_LIST are additional mirrors and don't affect the stored id. Must
# match the relay Worker's NOSTR_HOME_RELAY (same host). Defaults to
# wss://relay.pana.social.
# NOSTR_HOME_RELAY="wss://relay.pana.social"

# [VAR] The relay's hex x-only public key (non-sensitive). Used to label NIP-56
# abuse reports that target the relay itself in the moderation UI. Derive from
# the relay's secret key (nak — github.com/fiatjaf/nak):
#   nak key public <relay-nsec-or-hex-sec>
# RELAY_PUBLIC_KEY=""

# =============================================================================
# MOBILE APPS (Capacitor native shell -- see docs/MOBILE-ROADMAP.md)
# =============================================================================
# The Android and iOS apps are a WebView pointed at this deploy, not a second
# implementation of the site. Neither var below is needed to build or run an
# app -- they only make deep links (a pana.social URL tapped elsewhere) open in
# the app instead of the browser, which requires a signed build to exist first.
#
# Both are served publicly on purpose and are not secrets.

# [VAR] Comma-separated SHA-256 signing certificate fingerprints, served at
# /.well-known/assetlinks.json so Android verifies App Links.
#
# Expect at least TWO. With Play App Signing, Google re-signs the uploaded
# bundle with its own key, so what members install carries the Play signing
# certificate while internal-test and local builds carry the upload
# certificate. List one and links verify for half your builds -- usually the
# half you never test, because your own device runs the other. Both are shown
# under Release > Setup > App signing in the Play Console.
#
# Unset means the route 404s, which is the correct answer anywhere without a
# signed build: links just open in the browser.
# ANDROID_APP_CERT_FINGERPRINTS=""

# [VAR] Apple Developer Team ID, served at
# /.well-known/apple-app-site-association so iOS verifies Universal Links.
# On the membership page of the Apple Developer account. Unset means the route
# 404s and links open in Safari.
# APPLE_APP_TEAM_ID=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GIFs in direct messages
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [SECRET] GIPHY API key, from https://developers.giphy.com/dashboard/
# (create an account, then "Create an API Key"). Issued instantly.
#
# This was Tenor until Google decommissioned that API on 30 June 2026 and
# stopped issuing keys in January, so no Tenor key can be obtained at all.
#
# Beta keys -- which is what the dashboard gives you -- are capped at 100
# calls PER HOUR for the whole app, not per member. That is the tightest
# constraint in this feature, and the reason /api/social/gifs caches results
# for an hour in two tiers. A production key lifts the cap but needs an
# application and a pricing conversation with GIPHY.
#
# Unset is a supported state, not a broken one: /api/social/gifs answers
# { enabled: false } and the composer never draws the GIF button at all. The
# feature is off rather than visibly failing.
#
# Set it in production with:
#   npx wrangler secret put GIPHY_API_KEY
#
# GIPHY's API Terms of Service (clause 4) require the words "Powered By GIPHY"
# *and* their logo -- a conjunction, not a choice. The picker carries both; do
# not drop either to tidy the UI. Clause 5 separately requires crediting the
# uploader wherever GIPHY gives us one, which is why a GIF's creator rides
# along to the mirror route and into the attachment's alt text.
#
# COMPLIANCE NOTE: our integration proxies API calls through the Worker and
# mirrors chosen GIFs into R2. Both are discouraged by GIPHY's *developer
# documentation* ("do not proxy requests to GIPHY"; "do not cache... copies of
# GIPHY media assets") -- but neither prohibition appears anywhere in the API
# Terms of Service those docs link to, which is the document we actually
# agreed to. The terms do reserve GIPHY's right to impose limits "from time to
# time", so treat this as unresolved rather than settled in our favour. Either
# way the remedy they reserve is key revocation, not a bill. See the long note
# at the top of app/api/social/gifs/mirror/route.ts before changing this.
#
# Chosen GIFs are copied into our own R2 bucket on send rather than hotlinked.
# Hotlinking would tell GIPHY the IP address of everyone a GIF is sent to,
# every time the conversation is reopened -- and would leave a copy of the
# message's content on a third party's CDN after our own 30-day expiry has
# removed it here.
# GIPHY_API_KEY=""
